WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.478
This lesson depends on one separation: graph height answers the speed-now question, while graph slope answers the change-in-speed question.

2
00:00:05.478 --> 00:00:10.956
A speed-time graph is not mainly about where the object is.

3
00:00:10.956 --> 00:00:16.433
It is about how fast the object is moving at different times and whether that speed is staying the same, rising, or falling.

4
00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:21.911
Once height and slope are given separate jobs, the graph becomes much easier to read.

5
00:00:21.911 --> 00:00:27.389
Keep this takeaway in view: On a speed-time graph, height and slope answer different motion questions.

6
00:00:27.389 --> 00:00:32.867
Graph height means speed now.

7
00:00:32.867 --> 00:00:38.344
The vertical position gives the speed at that instant.

8
00:00:38.344 --> 00:00:43.822
Graph slope means acceleration.

9
00:00:43.822 --> 00:00:49.300
The tilt shows how the speed is changing over time.

10
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:55.159
The speed-time graph is read differently from the distance-time graph.

11
00:00:55.159 --> 00:01:01.018
The graph height tells the speed at a chosen instant.

12
00:01:01.018 --> 00:01:06.877
A high point means a high speed then.

13
00:01:06.877 --> 00:01:12.736
The slope tells whether the speed is increasing, decreasing, or staying constant over an interval.

14
00:01:12.736 --> 00:01:18.594
A flat line above zero means the object keeps moving with constant speed.

15
00:01:18.594 --> 00:01:24.453
That is one of the most important corrections this lesson makes, because many students see flat and say stopped automatically.

16
00:01:24.453 --> 00:01:30.312
Keep this takeaway in view: A speed-time graph is a pace log, not a map and not a position graph.

17
00:01:30.312 --> 00:01:36.171
Height: The line's level tells the speed at that instant.

18
00:01:36.171 --> 00:01:42.030
Slope: The tilt tells whether the speed is increasing or decreasing.

19
00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:46.587
A helpful analogy is a speedometer recording.

20
00:01:46.587 --> 00:01:51.143
The number shown at one instant tells the speed now.

21
00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:55.700
But if that readout rises across a few seconds, it means the speed is increasing.

22
00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.257
If it stays flat, the speed is staying constant.

23
00:02:00.257 --> 00:02:04.813
If it falls, the speed is decreasing.

24
00:02:04.813 --> 00:02:09.370
This analogy matters because it keeps the learner from treating the graph line as one single idea.

25
00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:13.927
It is a changing readout with a present value and a pattern of change.

26
00:02:13.927 --> 00:02:18.483
Keep this takeaway in view: Read the pace log like a changing speedometer record.

27
00:02:18.483 --> 00:02:23.040
Readout value means speed now.

28
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.597
What the dial says at one instant is the current speed.

29
00:02:27.597 --> 00:02:32.153
Readout trend means change in speed.

30
00:02:32.153 --> 00:02:36.710
Rising, flat, or falling tells the acceleration story.

31
00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:41.907
On a speed-time graph, the slope is change in speed divided by change in time.

32
00:02:41.907 --> 00:02:47.103
That is acceleration.

33
00:02:47.103 --> 00:02:52.300
So a steeper upward section means a larger positive acceleration.

34
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.497
A downward section means negative acceleration.

35
00:02:57.497 --> 00:03:02.693
A flat section has zero slope, so the acceleration is zero there.

36
00:03:02.693 --> 00:03:07.890
This is a crucial moment in the module because the same idea of steepness now carries a new physics meaning due to the new axes.

37
00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:13.087
Keep this takeaway in view: On a speed-time graph, slope answers the acceleration question.

38
00:03:13.087 --> 00:03:18.283
Upward slope: Speed is increasing, so acceleration is positive.

39
00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:23.480
Downward slope: Speed is decreasing, so acceleration is negative.

40
00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:28.677
acceleration = slope = change in speed / change in time.

41
00:03:28.677 --> 00:03:33.873
The graph type decides the slope meaning.

42
00:03:33.873 --> 00:03:39.070
Here it belongs to acceleration.

43
00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:44.019
A flat section above zero means steady motion.

44
00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:48.968
A rising section means speeding up.

45
00:03:48.968 --> 00:03:53.917
A falling section means slowing down if the graph is a speed-time graph and the speed stays positive.

46
00:03:53.917 --> 00:03:58.866
These three section types act like a grammar for graph reading.

47
00:03:58.866 --> 00:04:03.815
Before calculating anything, learners can already say a great deal about the motion story by naming whether each section is flat, rising, or falling and by checking the height of the line.

48
00:04:03.815 --> 00:04:08.764
Keep this takeaway in view: Name the section type first, then calculate only if the question needs a number.

49
00:04:08.764 --> 00:04:13.713
Flat means steady speed.

50
00:04:13.713 --> 00:04:18.662
The speed stays the same during that interval.

51
00:04:18.662 --> 00:04:23.611
Rising means speeding up.

52
00:04:23.611 --> 00:04:28.560
The line climbs because the speed value is increasing.

53
00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.925
One of the biggest mistakes in this lesson is mixing up zero slope with zero height.

54
00:04:32.925 --> 00:04:37.290
Zero slope means the graph is flat, so the speed is unchanging.

55
00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:41.655
But if that flat line sits above zero, the object is still moving.

56
00:04:41.655 --> 00:04:46.020
Rest would mean the graph height itself is zero.

57
00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.385
This lesson matters because it forces the learner to keep line level and line tilt as separate sources of meaning.

58
00:04:50.385 --> 00:04:54.750
Keep this takeaway in view: Never confuse a flat graph with a zero graph.

59
00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.115
Zero slope means zero acceleration.

60
00:04:59.115 --> 00:05:03.480
Zero height means zero speed.

61
00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.897
Check yourself.

62
00:05:06.897 --> 00:05:10.314
What does graph height mean on a speed-time graph?

63
00:05:10.314 --> 00:05:13.731
The speed at that instant.

64
00:05:13.731 --> 00:05:17.148
What does graph slope mean?

65
00:05:17.148 --> 00:05:20.565
The acceleration.

66
00:05:20.565 --> 00:05:23.982
And what does a flat line above zero mean?

67
00:05:23.982 --> 00:05:27.398
Constant speed, not rest.

68
00:05:27.398 --> 00:05:30.815
Those three answers are enough to unlock the worked example because the example is really asking you to read those roles cleanly in sequence.

69
00:05:30.815 --> 00:05:34.232
Keep this takeaway in view: Speed-time graphs become easy when the graph roles stay separate.

70
00:05:34.232 --> 00:05:37.649
Height means speed.

71
00:05:37.649 --> 00:05:41.066
This is the speed-now feature.

72
00:05:41.066 --> 00:05:44.483
Slope means acceleration.

73
00:05:44.483 --> 00:05:47.900
This is the change-in-speed feature.

74
00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:53.472
You are ready for the worked example now.

75
00:05:53.472 --> 00:05:59.043
A speed-time graph is flat at six metres per second for four seconds, then rises steadily to twelve metres per second by seven seconds.

76
00:05:59.043 --> 00:06:04.615
Read the flat section first: the object moves at constant speed of six metres per second.

77
00:06:04.615 --> 00:06:10.187
Then read the rising section: the speed increases from six to twelve, so the object is speeding up.

78
00:06:10.187 --> 00:06:15.758
Keep the meanings separate: graph height gives the speed value, while the upward slope shows positive acceleration.

79
00:06:15.758 --> 00:06:21.330
So the story is steady motion first, then speeding up.

80
00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:26.902
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: height tells the speed, slope tells the change.

81
00:06:26.902 --> 00:06:32.473
Step 1: Read the speed value from the graph height.

82
00:06:32.473 --> 00:06:38.045
Step 2: Read the change pattern from the slope.

83
00:06:38.045 --> 00:06:43.617
flat at 6 m/s -> constant speed.

84
00:06:43.617 --> 00:06:49.188
rise to 12 m/s -> speeding up.

85
00:06:49.188 --> 00:06:54.760
Tell the story by reading the section type and the line level together.
